1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,470
Good morning everyone. Today's lecture forms part of the Hospitality and Tourism module.

2
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:11,570
Last week I looked at the economy end of the hotel business;

3
00:00:11,570 --> 00:00:15,670
this week I'm going to discuss the luxury end of the market.

4
00:00:15,670 --> 00:00:19,160
Let's consider the following scenario...

5
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:26,300
You wake up in the middle of the night in a strange hotel miles away from home,

6
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:28,820
disoriented most probably from jet lag,

7
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:33,440
when even the most expensive surroundings can seem empty and dispiriting.

8
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:40,912
You have paid a great deal of money to stay in this first-class hotel with its contemporary technology,

9
00:00:40,912 --> 00:00:45,479
but according to recent research carried out by an international travel

10
00:00:45,479 --> 00:00:48,800
and public relations company, all is not well.

11
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:52,833
The research suggests that even the most opulent,

12
00:00:52,833 --> 00:01:02,412
luxurious hotels seem to have underestimated the most basic needs of their customers-be they travelling for work or pleasure:

13
00:01:02,412 --> 00:01:09,470
the need to feel at home in surroundings which are both familiar and inviting.

14
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:16,720
Do these findings, however, apply only to hotels situated in particular areas?

15
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:21,940
Is it possible that the external environment can affect a guest's well-being?

16
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:27,060
The company's research covered a whole range of different hotel types,

17
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:32,180
both independent hotels and those which are part of large chains.

18
00:01:32,180 --> 00:01:38,605
They investigated chic so-called boutique hotels in the heart of downtown business districts,

19
00:01:38,605 --> 00:01:43,054
stately mansions located in the depths of beautiful countryside,

20
00:01:43,054 --> 00:01:51,950
and plush hotels built at the edge of tropical beaches surrounded by palm trees and idyllic blue ocean.

21
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:59,440
And the research concluded that what was outside the hotel building simply didn't matter.

22
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:02,840
This is a fascinating revelation

23
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,640
and those of you hoping to move into careers in the travel

24
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:11,110
and leisure industry would be well advised to look at the findings in more detail.

25
00:02:11,110 --> 00:02:16,660
But back to the main point of this lecture... the need to feel at home.

26
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:20,640
What can the hotel industry do about it?

27
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:23,180
And is the very idea

28
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:28,050
so subjective that it's impossible to do anything about it on a global basis?

29
00:02:28,050 --> 00:02:32,540
However, nothing stands still in this world.

30
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:37,699
One company has come up with the slogan 'Take Your Home With You',

31
00:02:37,699 --> 00:02:41,270
and aims to provide clients with luxury serviced apartments.

32
00:02:41,270 --> 00:02:48,730
Those in the business travel industry maintain that these serviced apartments dispense with all the unwanted

33
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:52,760
and expensive hotel services that business travellers don't want

34
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:56,190
while maximising the facilities they do want.

35
00:02:56,190 --> 00:02:59,836
For example, not only sleeping and living accommodation,

36
00:02:59,836 --> 00:03:05,035
but also a sleek modern kitchen that allows guests to cook

37
00:03:05,477 --> 00:03:08,950
and entertain if they wish, at no additional cost

38
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:11,680
The attractions of such facilities are obvious

39
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:14,520
and it'll be interesting to see

40
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:18,700
whether the company manages to establish a trend all over the world

41
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:22,600
and make a lasting impact on the luxury accommodation market.

42
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:30,730
Now, finally I want to consider the psychology underpinning the traditional holiday hotel industry.

43
00:03:30,730 --> 00:03:38,824
As a hotelier, how do you go about attracting people to give up the security of their own home

44
00:03:38,824 --> 00:03:43,060
and entrust themselves to staying in a completely strange place

45
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:45,640
and sleeping in an unfamiliar bed?

46
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:53,560
Firstly, hotels exploit people's need to escape the predictability of their everyday lives.

47
00:03:53,560 --> 00:04:00,710
For a few days people can pretend they are free of responsibilities and can indulge themselves.

48
00:04:00,710 --> 00:04:06,694
Secondly, there is something very powerful in our need to be pampered and looked after,

49
00:04:06,694 --> 00:04:11,413
it's almost as if we return to being a baby,

50
00:04:11,514 --> 00:04:15,470
when everything was done for us and we felt safe and secure

51
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:20,549
And not far removed from this is the pleasure in being spoilt

52
00:04:20,549 --> 00:04:26,475
and given little treats-like the miniscule bottles of shampoo and tiny bars of soap,

53
00:04:26,475 --> 00:04:30,060
the chocolate on your pillow at night

54
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:33,670
and we actually forget that we are paying for it all!

55
00:04:33,670 --> 00:04:40,900
Next week, I'm going to look at eco-hotels, a fairly new phenomenon but increasingly popular...
